## Notes for the agent author

(Delete this section before committing.)

A good specialist agent:
- Has a tight scope (3-5 owned areas, not 10)
- Has explicit handoff targets for adjacent concerns (don't drift into other specialists' scopes)
- Has a `tools:` allowlist that's the minimum sufficient set (no `Bash` if pure file work; no `mcp__sentry__*` if not doing crash triage)
- Has a Definition of Done with concrete verifiable artifacts
- Cites at most 2 cross-links — agents drown in cross-links

A bad specialist agent:
- Overlaps another specialist's scope (causes orchestrator confusion)
- Has `tools: Read, Edit, MultiEdit, Bash, Grep, Glob, mcp__*__*` (too permissive — defeats the purpose)
- Has aspirational rules ("we should always...") instead of enforced ones
- Has no auto-loaded instruction file (leaves rules implicit)
